# pyburn: three hot paths in the raw reader -- quadratic, per-element, per-value.
#
# MEASURED, one T42 orbit at NLOWIO = 0 (2.4 GB raw), driven with the EXACT
# kwargs Model.run() -> Model.postprocess() forwards for a configured
# postprocessor -- namelist None, the project's 120-code `variables` list --
# unprofiled, same input file both ways:
#
#     as shipped        304.0 s     3,062,749 np.append calls
#     stage 1 (2026-08-18)  29.4 s          1 np.append call
#
#     10.3x, and 123 of 123 output variables BITWISE IDENTICAL.
#
# That reproduces the pipeline's own history. `continue_exoplasim.py` records
# 387 s of wall clock an orbit for NLOWIO = 0, measured 2026-08-17; this bench
# gives 81 s of model plus 304 s of pyburn = 385 s. After stage 1 the same orbit
# is 81 + 29 = 110 s.
#
# Stage 2 (2026-08-19) took the read the rest of the way, on a SYNTHETIC raw
# file of the same geometry -- T42, 10 levels, 77 single-level and 36 ten-level
# codes over 185 steps, 80,845 records of 128x64 float32, 2.65 GB -- because no
# raw file survives postprocessing to re-measure the real one against:
#
#     readallvariables, stage 1     30.53 s
#     readallvariables, stage 2      0.96 s
#
#     32x, and every variable identical in VALUE, SHAPE AND DTYPE, as is the
#     whole of readfile()'s output.
#
# The read was essentially the entire remaining cost, so postprocessing an
# orbit is now a second or two against 77 to 93 s of model.
#
# WHAT WAS WRONG. Three separate defects, found by profiling and confirmed by
# call counts rather than by reading:
#
# 1. THE READER WAS QUADRATIC. `readallvariables` accumulated each variable
#    with `variables[kcode] = np.append(variables[kcode],field)`, once per
#    record. np.append reallocates and copies the WHOLE accumulated array every
#    call, so reading N records costs O(N^2) in copied bytes. One orbit is ~81k
#    records and that single line was 246 s of the 363 s. Records are now
#    collected in a list per code and joined once with np.concatenate, which is
#    the identical result because np.append flattens anyway.
#
# 2. DERIVED VERTICAL FIELDS LOOPED PER GRID CELL. The omega/wap, wa and
#    streamfunction branches ran a triple loop over (time, lat, lon), calling
#    np.append twice and scipy cumulative_trapezoid once per CELL -- 1.49e6
#    iterations an orbit each. Now integrated on the level axis for the whole
#    array at once. Worth ~81 s of the total; the arithmetic per column is
#    unchanged.
#
# 3. EVERY VALUE IN THE FILE WAS BOXED INTO A PYTHON FLOAT. With (1) gone, what
#    was left was `struct.unpack(en+datalength*fmt, ...)`, which builds a tuple
#    of Python float objects -- 8192 of them for one T42 grid record, and there
#    are ~81k such records in an orbit, so of order 6.6e8 objects created and
#    destroyed per orbit only to be handed straight to np.asarray. The payload
#    is now decoded with np.frombuffer, which touches no Python objects at all,
#    in `_decoderecord`. The views it returns are promoted to float64 once per
#    code at the concatenate, and float64 is exactly what np.asarray gave the
#    old tuple, so values AND dtype are unchanged.
#
#    NUMBA WOULD NOT HAVE HELPED HERE and is not used: the hot call is
#    struct.unpack over a bytes object, which nopython mode cannot compile, and
#    once the loop is rewritten to hand numpy the bytes there is nothing left
#    for a JIT to do. The remaining stages are already compiled or free --
#    grid-mode variables pass through _transformvar untouched, and spectral ones
#    go to the f2py'd pyfft.sp2gp.
#
#    Two functions carrying the same reader pattern, `readvariablecode` and
#    `_gettimevar`, are fixed alongside it. NEITHER COULD EVER HAVE RUN: both
#    call `readrecord(fbuffer,n,en,ml)` without its `mf` argument and raise
#    TypeError immediately, and `readvariablecode` additionally did `if not
#    variable:` on an ndarray. Nothing in this project calls either. They now
#    run and agree with `readallvariables` record for record.
#
# A NOTE ON HOW THIS WAS NEARLY MISDIAGNOSED. Under cProfile the 6.0e6 tiny
# np.append calls in (2) appeared to cost 246 s, which is what the profiler
# charges for 6 million call events, not what numpy spends. Fixing (2) alone
# moved an unprofiled orbit from 363.5 s to 282.8 s. The 246 s was really the
# ~81k LARGE appends in (1), which the same profile also attributed to
# np.append. Call counts separated them; the profile alone did not.
#
# VERIFICATION. Stage 1: two full-orbit comparisons, both zero differing fields
# -- the production invocation above at 123 of 123 bitwise identical, and a
# heavier 216-code run driven through a burn7-style namelist at 281 of 281. That
# second path is NOT what the pipeline uses -- pyburn ignores `variables` when a
# namelist is given (pyburn.py, "Scrape namelist"), and only a hand-written
# harness passes one -- but it exercises `wa` and the streamfunction, which
# production never requests, so it is the wider correctness net of the two.
# Stage 2: 116 of 116 variables and all 118 refactored readfile() keys equal in
# value, shape and dtype against the stage-1 reader on the synthetic file, which
# is the same test with a right answer of zero difference.
#
# THE BASE IT APPLIES TO. Authored 2026-08-18 and extended 2026-08-19, against
# pyburn.py as shipped in the pinned ExoPlaSim 3.4.2 wheel. It touches pyburn.py
# and nothing else, adds no dependency, and changes no model source, so no
# binary is affected by its CONTENT -- rebuild_binaries.py hashes plasim/src
# only, and --verify reports all binaries current across this change.
